<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Viceroyalty of New Granada</h1><p class="st-meta">1717 CE – 1819 CE · capital Santa Fe de Bogotá</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish viceroyalty of northern South America, ruled from Bogotá, whose collapse in the wars of independence gave rise to Gran Colombia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> created the Viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> in 1717 out of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-kingdom-of-granada">New Kingdom of Granada</a> and other territories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, to defend the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast and improve administration. Abolished in 1723 for lack of revenue, it was permanently restored in 1739, covering modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-colombia">Colombia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Ecuador</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Panama</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Santa Fe de Bogotá</a>. Cartagena's fortifications withstood the British attack under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-vernon">Edward Vernon</a> in 1741, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blas-de-lezo">Blas de Lezo</a> and Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-de-eslava">Sebastián de Eslava</a>. Gold from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choco-department">Chocó</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioquia-department">Antioquia</a> was the main export. In 1777 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela</a> was separated as a captaincy general. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-comuneros-new-granada">Comunero revolt</a> of 1781 opposed new taxes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-botanical-expedition-to-new-granada">Royal Botanical Expedition of José Celestino Mutis</a> from 1783 trained a generation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> scientists.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A junta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> on 20 July 1810 began the movement for independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-morillo">Pablo Morillo</a> reconquered the territory in 1816, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar's</a> victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boyaca">Boyacá</a> in 1819 ended Spanish rule and led to the creation of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/viceroyalty-of-new-granada.jpg" alt="Viceroyalty of New Granada" width="320" height="321"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viceroyalty_of_New_Granada_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1717 CE – 1819 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santa Fe de Bogotá</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sebastián de Eslava, Antonio Caballero y Góngora, Antonio Amar y Borbón</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>, <a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, <a href="/countries/panama">Panama</a>, <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Virreinato de Nueva Granada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1768&amp;focus=viceroyalty-of-new-granada" aria-label="Viceroyalty of New Granada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2626 2004 640 512" role="img" ari